-- STATE-AS-LEDGER x T6: the split-poison cache design (smucclaw/l4-ide#914).
--
-- WRITES poison the WHNFWhen (temporal-fingerprint) cache: a thunk whose
-- force APPENDS to the ledger is cached as a plain WHNF (snapshot at first
-- force), so the RECORD fires exactly once no matter how many temporal
-- scopes reuse the shared binding. (Without the write-poison, a re-force
-- under a different scope appended a second `RECORD `x`` row.)
--
-- READS snapshot PER TEMPORAL SCOPE: a thunk whose force only RECALLs is
-- WHNFWhen-cached on the axes the read observed — an unchanged scope
-- re-serves the first-force value (clause-free programs keep the classic
-- one-binding-one-value sharing), while a DIFFERENT scope re-forces against
-- the current ledger and gets its own scope-correct answer (#914 §2B).
--
-- Everything here is INTRA-directive on purpose: cross-directive shapes are
-- masked by the per-directive heap rebuild ('forEachDirectiveFreshHeap').
--
-- The test harness pins the clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31T15:45:30Z.

"Etc/UTC"

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Write side: the RECORD fires exactly once per first force
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`stamped write` MEANS (RECORD `x` IS 100) PLUS DATE_SERIAL TODAY

-- expected: 0 (one shared binding, one first-force value used twice) and
-- exactly ONE `RECORD `x`` ledger row.
-- (un-poisoned: -1857 — override value minus a re-forced ambient value —
-- and TWO ledger rows)
#EVAL (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) `stamped write`) MINUS `stamped write`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Read side: a shared RECALL thunk snapshots PER TEMPORAL SCOPE (#914 §2B)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`recall or today` MEANS
  CONSIDER RECALL `x`
  WHEN JUST v THEN v
  WHEN NOTHING THEN DATE_SERIAL TODAY

-- expected: LIST 737789, 7, 7 — the first use forces under AS OF 2020 (cell
-- `x` unwritten, so the override serial 737789 = DATE_SERIAL of 2020-01-01);
-- the bare third use is a DIFFERENT temporal scope, so the shared thunk
-- re-forces against the current ledger and reads the meanwhile-written 7.
-- (pre-#914 this snapshotted per directive: LIST 737789, 7, 737789)
#EVAL LIST (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) `recall or today`), (RECORD `x` IS 7), `recall or today`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Control (contract #914 §4): clause-free — same scope, snapshot re-served
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`recall or zero` MEANS
  CONSIDER RECALL `x`
  WHEN JUST v THEN v
  WHEN NOTHING THEN 0

-- expected: LIST 0, 7, 0 — with NO clause anywhere, both uses of the shared
-- binding run in the one ambient scope: the fingerprint matches and the
-- first-force snapshot is re-served, so the meanwhile-written 7 stays
-- invisible to the shared thunk. Clause-free programs keep the classic
-- pre-bitemporal sharing semantics (and this guards against a never-cache
-- "fix", which would yield LIST 0, 7, 7 here).
#EVAL LIST `recall or zero`, (RECORD `x` IS 7), `recall or zero`
